---
import Prose from '../layouts/Prose.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, REPO_URL, contactHref } from '../site';
---
<Prose
  eyebrow="Legal"
  title="Terms"
  updated="10 September 2026"
  description="RigorRun is MIT-licensed software you run yourself. There is no account, no service and no subscription, so these terms cover the licence, the warranty position and what a verdict does and does not mean."
  lede="RigorRun is software you download and run yourself under the MIT licence. There is no hosted service, no account and no subscription, so these terms are about the software and the website — not about a relationship either of us has signed up to."
>
  <h2>Licence</h2>
  <p>
    The <code>rigorrun</code> package and its source are released under the MIT licence. The full
    text ships in the package and is in the <a href={REPO_URL}>repository</a>. You may use it
    commercially, modify it and redistribute it, subject to that licence.
  </p>

  <h2>No warranty</h2>
  <p>
    As the MIT licence states, the software is provided "as is", without warranty of any kind. That
    is worth restating in plain language given what this tool is for:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A passing verdict is a statement about the checks that ran, against the parts of your system
      RigorRun was able to read, at the moment it ran. It is not a guarantee that your agent is
      safe, correct or compliant.
    </li>
    <li>
      A verdict against a real system is <code>PARTIAL</code> by design, and a verdict from a browser
      connection is <code>OBSERVATIONAL</code>. Both are labelled on every result. Treating either as
      stronger than it says it is, is a misreading this documentation cannot prevent.
    </li>
    <li>
      RigorRun runs against systems you point it at, using credentials you supply, and it performs
      actions that change data. Point it at staging. Deciding what is safe to run it against is
      yours.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Acceptable use</h2>
  <p>
    <code>rigorrun verify</code> downloads and executes third-party packages in a container in order
    to observe them. Use it on software you are entitled to analyse. Publishing a finding about
    somebody else's server is your responsibility, and the record it produces carries the limits of
    what was actually established — please do not quote the finding without them.
  </p>

  <h2>The website</h2>
  <p>
    Content on rigorrun.xyz is provided for information. Figures on the evidence pages are generated
    by scripts in the repository and can be reproduced; they describe specific runs on specific days
    against specific versions, and nothing more.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    These terms may change as the product does. Material changes will appear in the changelog. If
    something here is unclear, <a href={contactHref(CONTACT_EMAIL)}>ask</a>.
  </p>
</Prose>
